From bb63c90ca5f4e1ff84901bf773f5b06ae74bd50a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: elliotttate <elliotttate@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 18:16:32 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 125/128] Smooth Motion: a cloth's strips blended vertex by
 vertex

Flags and the boat's sail move their vertices on the CPU each game frame
and draw them as strips through position arrays, under a matrix that hardly
moves. The in-between frame blends matrices, so it drew the cloth where the
next frame has it: cloth flapped at 30 FPS while everything around it moved
at 60.

The host can now tag a cloth's strips the way it tags a wake's (a draw
scope over a count of draws, BP 0x7C/0x7B), with bit 23 of the count saying
that the scope's draws index their positions rather than send them. gxcore
gives each such draw its scope and place, frame_interp keys it by them, and
keeps and blends its decoded positions one by one, as it does a particle's
(blend_positions()). The per-draw trace (DOL_AURORA_FRAME_INTERP_TRACE)
shows a draw's scope.

frame_interp_test has a cloth case: a strip whose free edge sways by 8 units
is drawn halfway (4) in the in-between frame, and a strip that stood still
is drawn as it is. In play it needs the host's tags (Wind Waker Recomp's
draw_tags.c, at dCloth_packet_c::draw and daHo_packet_c::draw), which take
effect with a game module that watches those addresses.
